---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "How to Calculate Percentages: Three Common Cases - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "How to work out a percentage of a number, what percentage one number is of another, and percentage change, with worked examples.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-calculate-percentages.html">
<aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
    <div class="wrap article">
      <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Maths</p>
      <h1>How to Calculate Percentages: Three Common Cases</h1>
      <p class="lead">Almost every percentage question is one of three types. Once you can spot which one you have, the calculation is short.</p>

      <h2>1. What is X% of Y?</h2>
      <p>Turn the percentage into a decimal by dividing by 100, then multiply.</p>
      <p><strong>Example:</strong> 15% of 200 = 0.15 × 200 = 30.</p>
      <p>This is the case for tips, discounts and sales tax.</p>

      <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>

      <h2>2. X is what percent of Y?</h2>
      <p>Divide the part by the whole, then multiply by 100.</p>
      <p><strong>Example:</strong> 45 out of 180 is 45 ÷ 180 × 100 = 25%.</p>
      <p>Use this for test scores, survey results and budget shares.</p>

      <h2>3. Percentage change from X to Y</h2>
      <p>Subtract the starting value from the new one, divide by the starting value, then multiply by 100. A positive result is an increase and a negative one is a decrease.</p>
      <p><strong>Example:</strong> from 80 to 100 is (100 − 80) ÷ 80 × 100 = +25%.</p>
      <p>Always divide by the starting value, not the new one. Going from 100 to 80 is a 20% drop, but going from 80 to 100 is a 25% rise.</p>

      <h2>Quick checks</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>10% is the same as moving the decimal point one place left.</li>
        <li>A 50% increase and a 50% decrease do not cancel out. 100 up 50% is 150, and 150 down 50% is 75.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Try it yourself</h2>
      <p>The <a href="../tools/percentage-calculator.html">Percentage Calculator</a> answers all three questions as you type.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>Three quick examples: 15% of 200 is 0.15 × 200 = 30. 45 out of 180 is 45 ÷ 180 × 100 = 25%. A change from 80 to 100 is (100 − 80) ÷ 80 × 100 = +25%.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>What is the difference between a percentage change and percentage points?</summary><p>If a rate goes from 5% to 6%, it rose by one percentage point, which is a 20% increase on the original rate.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why does 50% up followed by 50% down not return to the start?</summary><p>The second change applies to a bigger number. 100 up 50% is 150, and 150 down 50% is 75.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do I find what percentage one number is of another?</summary><p>Divide the part by the whole, then multiply by 100.</p></details>
      </section>
